---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('privy')!;
const path = '/apps/privy';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/privy');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/privy');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Privy only a popup tool?",
    "answer": "No. Current supplier material documents email, SMS and visual Privy Flows alongside onsite displays. Evaluate the actual connected account for the journey you need."
  },
  {
    "question": "Did Privy close after the acquisition?",
    "answer": "Privy announced that it acquired Sendlane. That announcement does not establish a Privy shutdown or automatic account transfer; confirm account-specific continuity with support."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I add another sender at 5,000 contacts?",
    "answer": "No universal contact or order threshold establishes a need for a second sender. Demonstrate an unmet requirement and compare its benefit with the added operating cost."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I price the stack?",
    "answer": "Obtain current account terms for required features, audience, email, SMS, overages and any retained sender. Historical pageview and entry prices are not a current quote."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will every signup be a new subscriber?",
    "answer": "No. Test duplicate and existing identities, source mapping and channel eligibility separately. Count net new eligible contacts rather than all submissions."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a discount offer create duplicate welcomes?",
    "answer": "It can if multiple journeys own the same entry. Assign one owner, test retries and purchase suppression, and reconcile offer rules across displays and queued messages."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the economics measured Privy results?",
    "answer": "No. The contribution example is hypothetical and the acceptance fixtures are proposed work. Measure incremental outcomes before claiming lift."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare other platforms?",
    "answer": "Use the same capture, offer, identity and follow-up fixtures with each candidate. Keep Privy or the existing sender when it meets the brief at acceptable operating cost."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Evaluate onsite capture, email, SMS and visual flows as one customer journey. The practical decision is who owns identity, incentives and follow-up, with no prescribed second sender.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Email, SMS and onsite capture in one evaluation</h2>
      <p>Privy deserves evaluation as a marketing platform, rather than being confined to a popup layer. Its current <a href="https://www.privy.com/changelog">changelog</a> documents visual Privy Flows, email and SMS work, display editing, and Shopify order-related triggers. These are genuine reasons to evaluate capture and follow-up together. They do not establish a particular recovery rate or comparative rank.</p>
      <p>Choose between using Privy for both capture and messaging, or keeping an existing sender, by tracing one real subscriber journey. Record the form, identity, offer, recipient state and message owner. A second platform is useful only when it supplies an unmet requirement worth its integration and operating effort. There is no universal list-size, revenue or order-count boundary.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Sendlane acquisition and account continuity</h2>
      <p>Privy <a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">announced its acquisition of Sendlane in February 2026</a>. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> presents a combined direction for ecommerce marketing. Acquisition is not evidence that Privy has closed or that every Sendlane account automatically becomes a Privy account.</p>
      <p>Existing customers should obtain their account-specific continuity plan: login, contract, billing, support contact, feature access and any scheduled change. Export an inventory of forms, audiences, offers and active queues before an agreed transition. Preserve available permission records and suppression history. Do not recreate a live flow merely because a corporate announcement mentions a unified platform.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Capture design by visitor intent</h2>
      <p>For an influencer landing page, propose a collection-specific invitation with one clear benefit; for educational traffic, compare a useful guide with a discount. For returning buyers, consider suppressing acquisition offers. These are design choices to evaluate, not reported conversion improvements or guaranteed targeting controls.</p>
      <p>Preview the display on a small phone and desktop. Check close controls, keyboard navigation, cart obstruction, loading behavior and competing theme forms. Keep a record of where each display is eligible and which other capture surfaces remain active. A visitor should not encounter two contradictory incentives because two teams own different popup tools.</p>
      <p>Run one creative comparison at a time on comparable traffic. Record eligible impressions, completed signups, net new eligible contacts, offer redemptions and downstream contribution. Include existing subscribers and accidental resubmissions in separate counts; a successful submission is not necessarily a new audience member.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Identity and permission acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>Proposed fixture: submit a new staff address, submit it again with a different capitalization, and then submit an existing customer address. Inspect the resulting contact and source history. Record whether identity merges as intended, whether a welcome entry repeats, and whether the submitted collection interest survives. Do not assume every custom field or Shopify tag maps automatically.</p>
      <p>Use separate staff fixtures for email-only permission, SMS-only permission where supported, and withdrawal after signup. Inspect the actual channel state before each scheduled send. A phone number collected for another purpose is not by itself an SMS marketing subscription. A contact appearing in Shopify or a downstream sender does not prove eligibility.</p>
      <p>If another sender is retained, document field names, allowed values, transfer direction and failure handling. Test a delayed sync and a retry. The acceptance requirement is one eligible welcome entry with source metadata intact, rather than a promised fifteen-minute transfer time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Offer delivery and creative QA</h2>
      <p>Proposed jewelry-store fixture: compare a guide invitation with a discount invitation on the same collection. Choose the maximum discount from margin economics before designing a wheel or form. Confirm available display and coupon controls in the account instead of assuming every offer type is included.</p>
      <p>Follow the complete offer path: submission, confirmation, message, code application and checkout. Try an existing code, an expired code, an excluded product and an attempted stack with another promotion. Check whether the stated minimum spend and expiry agree across the form, email and checkout. A working email link is insufficient if the promised offer fails at payment.</p>
      <p>For creative review, check image crops, missing product variants, fallback text, links and mobile readability. Maintain an approved offer sheet with the owner, audience, exclusions and expiry. When an offer ends, update every display and queued message that still mentions it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Visual flows and purchase-aware follow-up</h2>
      <p>Build a proposed welcome path from the actual entry trigger and available conditions in Privy Flows. Draw the delay and intended audience for each message before activation. Test a subscriber who purchases during the delay and a subscriber who withdraws permission. Inspect pending entries as well as the first test message.</p>
      <p>For a cart journey, use staff activity to distinguish adding a product, starting checkout and completing purchase. Record which event actually enters the configured flow. Revisit the cart, change a variant and purchase from another device under the same identity. Verify the final product link and current purchase state rather than inventing a recovery percentage.</p>
      <p>For post-purchase or winback requirements, demonstrate the relevant trigger, order data and audience conditions in the connected account. Gift buyers, refunds and repeat buyers need an explicit message policy. Current flow scope makes a blanket claim that Privy cannot serve lifecycle work misleading; a difficult requirement still needs its own acceptance check.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Queue ownership when keeping another sender</h2>
      <p>Assign an owner for each display, welcome offer, campaign audience and recovery queue. If Privy captures while another sender welcomes, disable competing welcome entries for that cohort. If Privy owns the complete journey, retire the redundant form and flow only after verifying the replacement.</p>
      <p>Before a cutover, stop new incumbent entries and cancel or drain pending messages. Record which offers remain valid for queued contacts. Test replay of the same signup and cart event; retrying a transfer must not create an extra incentive or send. Recheck purchase, permission and suppression at send time.</p>
      <p>Rollback means naming who can pause the replacement and what happens to contacts already queued. Do not restart the old journey from its beginning for everyone. Use a bounded cohort with documented exclusions while proving the handoff.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Pricing worksheet and capture economics</h2>
      <p>Obtain a current quote for the connected account: audience basis, email volume, SMS credits and recipient regions, display access, required automation features, overages and contract terms. Historical contact/pageview tiers are not a reliable current bill. If a second sender remains, include both invoices and integration labor.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical planning example: 500 genuinely new eligible subscribers at $0.60 acquisition operating cost each cost $300. If a controlled comparison establishes 20 additional orders at $60 and 50% contribution margin before campaign expense, contribution is $600. Subtract $120 incremental discounts, $100 messaging cost and $80 labor as well as the $300 capture cost: net contribution is $0. These are assumptions, not Privy results.</p>
      <p>With zero incremental orders, those assumed expenses total $600. Attributed welcome revenue alone cannot establish that capture or a second sender paid back. Separate acquisition spend already committed from new experiment costs so the comparison answers the decision actually being made.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Rollout and operating handoff</h2>
      <p>Start with one display and one audience. Save the current theme/form configuration, offer sheet and active-flow inventory. Resolve staff identity, coupon and queue fixtures before allowing a bounded live cohort. Expand only when event handling and operator ownership are clear; a fixed ninety-day trial is not a substitute for these checks.</p>
      <p>After each creative change, inspect impressions by page and device alongside eligible contact growth and purchase outcomes. Investigate duplicate signups and suppression failures before celebrating list growth. During promotions, review code availability, SMS credit status and scheduled offers with the person who can pause them.</p>
      <p>The handoff should include display locations, creative versions, field mapping, permission evidence location, offer rules, message owners and rollback steps. Name the person who reviews stale incentives and the person who handles a failed integration. This keeps capture design connected to the customer experience after signup.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Fit and related decisions</h2>
      <p>Privy is a plausible candidate when a team wants onsite conversion and messaging in a connected workspace, or wants more deliberate capture while preserving a working sender. Demonstrate any specialized data, reporting or branching requirement before consolidating. Existing tooling that already meets the brief may be cheaper to keep.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/apps/shopify-email">native Shopify review</a> examines configuration and automation requirements before adding another sender. The <a href="/compare/privy-vs-justuno">Justuno comparison</a> is a capture-design decision; the <a href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">Omnisend</a>, <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-privy">Klaviyo</a> and <a href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy">Sequenzy comparisons</a> are prompts to demonstrate the same required journey, rather than instructions to buy a prescribed stack.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="link" href="/apps">All Shopify email apps →</a>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
